>> title: Kachumber
>> category: Salads
>> tags: indian, onion, cucumber, tomato, salad
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: kachumbar, cachumber, onion salad, indian onion salad, laccha pyaz, raw onion salad
>> pairs-with: papadom, seekh-kabab, dansak
>> servings: 6
>> time: 25 min
>> step.1: soak 10 min in ice water, then drain hard
>> step.2: salt and stand 10 min, then tip off the water
>> step.3: toss just before it goes out

Soak @red onion{1%large}(halved and sliced as thin as you can) in @ice water{2%cup}(475 mL) ~soak{10%min}, then drain it and press it dry in a cloth. Ten minutes in cold water takes the burn out of a raw onion and leaves the sweetness, and it is the difference between this and a plate nobody finishes.

Rest @cucumber{1%large}(seeded and diced) and @tomatoes{2%medium}(seeded and diced) under @kosher salt{1%tsp} ~stand{10%min}, then tip the pool of water off.

Toss @&(~1)drained cucumber and tomato{} with @&(~2)soaked onion{}, @green chile{1}(seeded, sliced fine), @cilantro{3%Tbs}(chopped), @lime juice{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @chaat masala{1/2%tsp} and @black pepper{1/4%tsp}, and put it on the table straight away.

This is the plate of raw onion that comes with the papadoms, and it does a job — cold, sharp and crunchy against ten sauces that are none of those things. It is also what goes into a naan with a seekh kabab. Dressed, it has about twenty minutes before it goes limp and watery, so the salting and the tossing are deliberately at opposite ends of the table.
